[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Bootstrap Lifecycle Events and DOM Manipulation via JavaScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Bootstrap components are dynamic and interactive, driven largely by custom JavaScript events and the Document Object Model (DOM). Understanding how to listen to component lifecycle events and programmatically manipulate the DOM allows developers to build highly responsive, customized web applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Bootstrap Component Lifecycles"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Most interactive Bootstrap components (such as Modals, Carousels, Collapse, and Tooltips) emit lifecycle events. These events generally follow a predictable pattern: a 'show' or 'hide' event fires when an action begins, and a 'shown' or 'hidden' event fires once the transition is completely finished."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"show.bs.modal: Fires immediately when the show instance method is called."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"shown.bs.modal: Fired when the modal has been made visible to the user and CSS transitions have completed."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"hide.bs.modal: Fires immediately when the hide instance method has been called."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"hidden.bs.modal: Fired when the modal has finished being hidden from the user."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Listening to Bootstrap Events in Vanilla JavaScript"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To hook into these events, use standard JavaScript event listeners attached to the specific element containing the Bootstrap component attribute data-bs-toggle."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"const myModalEl = document.getElementById('myModal');
myModalEl.addEventListener('hidden.bs.modal', event => {
  console.log('The modal is now completely hidden.');
  // Perform cleanup or DOM manipulation here
});"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Programmatic DOM Manipulation with Bootstrap Instances"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Instead of relying solely on HTML markup and data attributes, you can initialize and control Bootstrap components entirely through JavaScript. This gives you exact programmatic control over when components trigger."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Initialize a modal via JS and show it programmatically
const modalElement = document.querySelector('#dynamicModal');
const modalInstance = bootstrap.Modal.getOrCreateInstance(modalElement);

// Trigger the show method
modalInstance.show();"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Dynamic Content Injection into DOM Elements"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Combining lifecycle events with DOM manipulation is extremely powerful when fetching dynamic data. For example, you can clear a form or inject data into a modal body right before the modal becomes visible."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"const loadDataModal = document.getElementById('dataModal');

loadDataModal.addEventListener('show.bs.modal', event => {
  // Button that triggered the modal
  const button = event.relatedTarget;
  // Extract info from data-* attributes
  const recipient = button.getAttribute('data-bs-whatever');

  // Manipulate the DOM
  const modalTitle = loadDataModal.querySelector('.modal-title');
  const modalBodyInput = loadDataModal.querySelector('.modal-body input');

  modalTitle.textContent = 'New message to ' + recipient;
  modalBodyInput.value = recipient;
});"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Memory Management"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"When dynamically creating Bootstrap components like Tooltips or Popovers via JavaScript, always ensure you dispose of them properly when they are removed from the DOM to prevent memory leaks."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"const tooltipElement = document.getElementById('exampleTooltip');
const tooltipInstance = new bootstrap.Tooltip(tooltipElement);

// Later, during cleanup phase:
tooltipInstance.dispose();"}]
